{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 New York Yankees opened their season on a sour note, surrendering a grand slam on Opening Day and stumbling through an early April stretch plagued by bullpen miscues. Devin Williams, brought in to be the late-inning anchor, instead became a recurring source of heartbreak, coughing up leads with alarming regularity. By mid-spring, the Yankees looked like a team still searching for its identity, relying on scattered offensive flashes from young players like Jasson Dom\u00ednguez to stay afloat.\n\nThe summer months transformed the narrative. Trent Grisham emerged as an unlikely power threat, capping his breakout with a grand slam in July that electrified the Bronx. Anthony Volpe and Cody Bellinger delivered back-to-back clutch moments on July 30th that swung a pivotal game, and Austin Wells showed he could be a force behind the plate both offensively and in the clubhouse. The lineup, anchored by Aaron Judge and bolstered by Paul Goldschmidt and Jazz Chisholm Jr., rattled off 849 runs on the season \u2014 a testament to a deep and dangerous offense.\n\nAugust brought turbulence. David Bednar's double and Camilo Doval's late implosion represented the kind of bullpen volatility that had haunted this team all year, and the Yankees were forced to lean harder on their starting rotation and their potent offense to paper over the cracks. Still, they steadied the ship through September, with a late-season comeback \u2014 capped by Cody Bellinger drawing a walk and Jos\u00e9 Caballero singling home the go-ahead run \u2014 clinching the division and sending New York into the postseason with momentum.\n\nThe playoffs, however, told a crueler story. Luke Weaver faltered in the Wild Card round, surrendering back-to-back hits that handed the opposition a critical lead before the offense rallied to advance. In the ALDS, Aaron Judge delivered a three-run blast to give the Yankees early hope, but Devin Williams \u2014 the symbol of the bullpen's fragility all season \u2014 gave it right back, allowing two runs on a Nathan Lukes single that proved decisive. The Yankees' 94-win season, full of drama and resilience, ended one round too soon, leaving a roster brimming with talent hungry for another shot.",
